 "signs": [
  {
   "text": "INVERNESS ICE RINK. Home to curling, skating and one Keeper who will not be hurried. Curling is 'the roaring game' because of the sound the granite stones make on the ice. Sweeping is allowed. Shouting is encouraged.",
   "x": 53.0,
   "y": 18.0
  },
  {
   "text": "THE VICTORIAN MARKET. Built in 1870 and rebuilt after a fire in 1889, with an iron roof that keeps the rain off the tablet. Scottish tablet is sugar, butter and condensed milk. Mind your fillings.",
   "x": 47.0,
   "y": 33.0
  },
  {
   "text": "THE CAMANACHD PITCH. Shinty is the Highland game, a cousin of Irish hurling, played with a curved stick called a caman. Two teams of twelve. Everyone swings. Everyone says it was an accident.",
   "x": 12.0,
   "y": 31.0
  },
  {
   "text": "THE MORAY FIRTH. Home to about 200 bottlenose dolphins, among the biggest of their kind in the world, and they jump for fun. At Chanonry Point they do it on the rising tide, in front of a crowd with cameras.",
   "x": 15.0,
   "y": 7.0
  },
  {
   "text": "THE RIVER NESS (Abhainn Nis). Only about 10 km long, which makes it one of the shortest rivers in Britain, but it carries all of Loch Ness down to the sea. The town's Gaelic name, Inbhir Nis, means 'mouth of the Ness'.",
   "x": 21.0,
   "y": 9.0
  },
  {
   "text": "THE NESS ISLANDS. A park on a string of river islands, linked by Victorian footbridges since 1853. The trees are huge. The bridges wobble gently, as a hint.",
   "x": 21.0,
   "y": 35.0
  },
  {
   "text": "INVERNESS CASTLE. The pink sandstone castle was built in the 1830s as a courthouse and jail, on a fortress site nearly a thousand years old. The statue is Flora MacDonald, who helped Bonnie Prince Charlie escape.",
   "x": 34.0,
   "y": 14.0
  },
  {
   "text": "ST ANDREW'S CATHEDRAL. Built in the 1860s, it was the first new cathedral in Britain since the Reformation. The two towers were meant to carry spires, which never came, so they look a bit shy.",
   "x": 10.0,
   "y": 29.0
  },
  {
   "text": "INBHIR NIS · INVERNESS. 'Capital of the Highlands' since 565 AD, when St Columba came to visit the Pictish king Brude and had a word about a monster on the way. Fàilte.",
   "x": 30.0,
   "y": 21.0
  },
  {
   "x": 31,
   "y": 19,
   "text": "BLUE PLAQUE: In February 1746 Jacobite troops blew up the fort at Inverness, a few weeks before the Battle of Culloden.",
   "kind": "plaque",
   "dx": -0.475,
   "plaque": "inverness_old_fort",
   "title": "The Old Fort"
  },
  {
   "x": 38,
   "y": 12,
   "plaque": "inverness_macbeth",
   "title": "The Real Macbeth",
   "text": "Shakespeare set King Duncan's murder in Macbeth's castle at Inverness. The real Macbeth killed Duncan in battle in 1040, near Elgin, then ruled Scotland for seventeen years and even made a pilgrimage to Rome. History has been trying to show his good side ever since."
  },
  {
   "x": 45,
   "y": 41,
   "plaque": "inverness_culloden",
   "title": "Culloden",
   "text": "On 16 April 1746, on Drumossie Moor east of town, the Jacobite army of Charles Edward Stuart met the Duke of Cumberland's government troops. It was over in about an hour. Stones on the field still mark the graves of the clans, and people still leave flowers beside them."
  },
  {
   "x": 12,
   "y": 7,
   "plaque": "inverness_red_kites",
   "title": "Red Kites",
   "text": "Red kites were persecuted to extinction in Scotland more than a century ago. From 1989, birds from Sweden were released on the Black Isle, just across the firth, and they settled in. Look for a forked tail and a lazy, tilting glide. They are scavengers, and they have opinions about your picnic."
  },
  {
   "x": 4,
   "y": 28,
   "plaque": "inverness_tomnahurich",
   "title": "Tomnahurich",
   "text": "The wooded hill by the canal is Tomnahurich, from the Gaelic Tom na h-Iubhraich: 'hill of the yew wood', or perhaps 'of the boat', for its upturned-hull shape. Legend says the fairies lived inside it, and that fiddlers who went in for one night came out a hundred years later. It is a cemetery now."
  },
  {
   "x": 47,
   "y": 6,
   "text": "THE KESSOCK BRIDGE. North over the firth to the Black Isle, the Flow Country, Thurso and John o' Groats. The bridge opened in 1982 and is a favourite spot for watching the dolphins, if you can stop to look."
  }
 ],
